# Line endings, stated rather than left to whoever cloned the repository.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine setting, so without this file the same commit
# checks out with different bytes on different machines. That matters for exactly
# two kinds of file here:
#
#   * `gradlew` is a POSIX shell script. A CRLF copy of it fails on Linux and
#     macOS with `$'\r': command not found`, which reads like a broken Gradle
#     rather than a broken checkout.
#   * `gradlew.bat` is a Windows batch file, where the reverse is true: some
#     interpreters mis-parse an LF-only batch file.
#
# The rest of the tree is text and is pinned to LF so a diff never shows a whole
# file changed because one machine disagrees with another.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files keep CRLF on every platform.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts keep LF even on Windows, where they are run by Git Bash or WSL.
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary files are never converted.
*.jar binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.xls binary
*.zip binary
*.apk binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
